    <strong>Gender Gap:</strong> The global male-to-female homicide rate ratio stands at {kpis['male_female_ratio']:.1f}:1, meaning men are killed at more than three times the rate of women. This disparity has been remarkably persistent over the three-decade observation period. Male median rates consistently exceed 4–5 per 100,000, while female rates remain below 2 per 100,000 throughout.
</div>

<p>While the ratio has remained relatively stable, both male and female rates have shown a gradual downward trend over time. The absolute gap between male and female rates has narrowed slightly, but proportionally the disparity persists. This pattern is consistent across regions, though the magnitude of the gap varies — countries with high overall homicide rates tend to have particularly elevated male rates, often driven by gang-related and criminal violence that disproportionately affects young men.</p>

