    .badge-neutral {{ background: #e9ecef; color: #7f8c8d; }}
    .footer {{
        background: var(--dark);
        color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7);
        padding: 24px 50px;
        font-size: 0.85em;
    }}
    @media (max-width: 768px) {{
        .header, .content, .footer {{ padding-left: 20px; padding-right: 20px; }}
        .kpi-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }}
    }}
    @media (max-width: 480px) {{
        .kpi-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
    }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="report-container">

<!-- ═══ HEADER ═══ -->
<div class="header">
    <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends:<br>A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023)</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of intentional homicide rates across countries, sexes, and age groups over three decades, drawing on UNODC data.</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: UNODC Crime Trends Survey | Dataset: {R['overview']['unique_countries']} countries | Period: {R['overview']['year_range'][0]}–{R['overview']['year_range'][1]} | {R['overview']['total_rows']:,} observations</div>
</div>

<div class="content">

<!-- ═══ TABLE OF CONTENTS ═══ -->
<h2>Table of Contents</h2>
<div class="toc">
    <a href="#kpi-dashboard">Executive KPI Dashboard</a>
    <a href="#global-overview">1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</a>
    <a href="#regional-comparison">2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</a>
    <a href="#sex-disparity">3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</a>
    <a href="#age-group-analysis">4. Age Group Analysis</a>
    <a href="#notable-trends">5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</a>
    <a href="#data-limitations">6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</a>
</div>

<!-- ═══ EXECUTIVE KPI DASHBOARD ═══ -->
<h2 id="kpi-dashboard">Executive KPI Dashboard</h2>
<div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['global_median_homicide_rate']:.2f}</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Homicide Rate<br>(per 100,000, latest year)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card highlight">
        <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['male_female_ratio']:.2f}:1</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Homicide Rate Ratio<br>(global median)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warning">
        <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['highest_national_rate']['rate']:.1f}</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate<br>({kpis['highest_national_rate']['country']}, {kpis['highest_national_rate']['year']})</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card success">
        <div class="kpi-value">–9.6%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">2000s → 2010s Change<br>(decade-over-decade)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.1f}%</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Latest Reporting Coverage<br>({kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_year']})</div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Headline Finding:</strong> The global median homicide rate stands at {kpis['global_median_homicide_rate']:.2f} per 100,000 population. Males are victimized at {kpis['male_female_ratio']:.1f}× the rate of females. The world saw a notable decline in homicide rates from the 2000s to the 2010s (–9.6%), though the trajectory into the 2020s has been essentially flat (+0.2%). Reporting coverage remains a significant concern, with only {kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.0f}% of countries providing data in the most recent year.
</div>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW ═══ -->
<h2 id="global-overview">1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>

<p>This section examines the evolution of intentional homicide rates worldwide from 1990 to 2023. We use the median as the primary central-tendency measure because the distribution of homicide rates is heavily right-skewed — a small number of extremely high-rate countries would disproportionately inflate the mean. The number of countries reporting data each year is shown alongside the rate trends, as coverage variations directly affect the comparability of annual figures.</p>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart1_b64)}
</div>

<h3>Key Observations</h3>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Trend Summary:</strong> The global median homicide rate has declined from approximately {gt['median_rate'][0]:.1f} per 100,000 in {gt['years'][0]} to {gt['median_rate'][-1]:.1f} in {gt['years'][-1]}, representing a meaningful long-term improvement. However, the mean rate consistently exceeds the median by a large margin, reflecting the heavy-tailed distribution where a handful of countries experience extraordinarily high rates. Reporting coverage expanded significantly from the 2000s onward, which affects the comparability of figures across the entire period.
</div>

<p>The gap between mean and median rates is structurally important. In recent years, the mean has been roughly 2–3× the median, indicating that a small subset of high-violence countries pulls the average substantially upward. This means that while the "typical" country has become safer, the experience of extreme violence persists in specific settings.</p>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart10_b64)}
</div>

<p>The decade-over-decade analysis reveals that the 2000s saw a slight increase (+2.9%) compared to the 1990s baseline, followed by a meaningful decline in the 2010s (–9.6%). The 2020s have shown near-stagnation (+0.2%), suggesting that the gains of the prior decade may be leveling off. This plateau warrants close monitoring, as it could indicate that the "low-hanging fruit" of homicide reduction has been captured and further progress may require more targeted interventions.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON ═══ -->
<h2 id="regional-comparison">2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>

<p>Homicide rates vary enormously across regions and countries. This section identifies the most and least affected nations and examines geographic patterns. Regional medians are used to account for the wide variation in country-level rates within each region.</p>

<h3>Regional Median Rates</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart2_b64)}
</div>

<p>The Americas stand out dramatically, with a regional median homicide rate far exceeding all other regions. This is driven by persistently high rates in Central and South America, where organized crime, gang violence, and weak rule of law contribute to elevated victimization. Africa shows the second-highest regional median, with substantial variation across the continent. In contrast, Europe and Asia maintain comparatively low median rates, though important outliers exist within these regions.</p>

<h3>Regional Trends Over Time</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart3_b64)}
</div>

<p>The regional trend chart reveals that the Americas' elevated rates have been persistent across the entire observation period, with only modest declines. Africa's trajectory has been more volatile, partly reflecting changes in reporting coverage. Europe has maintained the most stable and low-level trajectory, while Asia has seen a gradual decline. Oceania's rates remain low and stable throughout.</p>

<h3>Top 10 Highest &amp; Lowest Homicide Rate Countries</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart4_b64)}
</div>

<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:20px; margin:16px 0;">
<div>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{country_table(R['top10_countries'], 'high')}</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{country_table(R['bottom10_countries'], 'low')}</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>

<p>The disparity between the highest and lowest rates is staggering — a difference of more than two orders of magnitude. The top 10 countries are predominantly in the Americas and Caribbean, while the lowest rates are concentrated in East Asia and the Middle East. It should be noted that some small island territories appear in these rankings; their rates can be volatile due to small population denominators.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY ═══ -->
<h2 id="sex-disparity">3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>

<p>One of the most striking features of global homicide patterns is the pronounced gender disparity. Men are far more likely to die by homicide than women in virtually every country and time period. The male-to-female homicide rate ratio is a critical KPI that quantifies this gap.</p>

<div class="kpi-grid" style="max-width:400px;">
    <div class="kpi-card highlight">
        <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['male_female_ratio']:.2f}:1</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Global Male-to-Female Ratio<br>(median across country-years)</div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart5_b64)}
</div>

<h3>Key Findings</h3>

<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Gender Gap:</strong> The global male-to-female homicide rate ratio stands at {kpis['male_female_ratio']:.1f}:1, meaning men are killed at more than three times the rate of women. This disparity has been remarkably persistent over the three-decade observation period. Male median rates consistently exceed 4–5 per 100,000, while female rates remain below 2 per 100,000 throughout.
</div>

<p>While the ratio has remained relatively stable, both male and female rates have shown a gradual downward trend over time. The absolute gap between male and female rates has narrowed slightly, but proportionally the disparity persists. This pattern is consistent across regions, though the magnitude of the gap varies — countries with high overall homicide rates tend to have particularly elevated male rates, often driven by gang-related and criminal violence that disproportionately affects young men.</p>

<h3>Countries with Highest Male-to-Female Ratios</h3>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Male Rate</th><th>Female Rate</th><th>M/F Ratio</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{mf_table(R['gender']['top_mf_countries'])}</tbody>
</table>

<p>In the countries with the highest M/F ratios, male homicide rates can exceed female rates by factors of 8 or more. These extreme ratios typically occur in countries experiencing armed conflict, organized crime activity, or both — settings where young men are disproportionately involved in violence as both perpetrators and victims.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 4: AGE GROUP ANALYSIS ═══ -->
<h2 id="age-group-analysis">4. Age Group Analysis</h2>

<p>Homicide risk is not uniformly distributed across age groups. Understanding which populations face the greatest risk is essential for targeting prevention interventions effectively.</p>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart6_b64)}
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Peak Risk Age:</strong> Young adults aged 15–29 consistently face the highest homicide rates globally, with a median rate of {R['age_groups']['median_rates'].get('15-29', 'N/A')} per 100,000. This is followed by the 30–44 age group at {R['age_groups']['median_rates'].get('30-44', 'N/A')} per 100,000. Children aged 0–14 have the lowest rates at {R['age_groups']['median_rates'].get('0-14', 'N/A')} per 100,000.
</div>

<p>The age-gradient pattern is remarkably consistent across countries and time periods. The concentration of risk among 15–29 year-olds aligns with what criminological research identifies as the peak ages for both violent offending and victimization. This age group is overrepresented in gang membership, street-level drug markets, and other high-risk environments. The decline in rates among older age groups likely reflects both reduced exposure to high-risk situations and life-course desistance from violence.</p>

<h3>Age Group Trends Over Time</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart7_b64)}
</div>

<p>The trend analysis by age group shows that all age groups have experienced some decline over the observation period, but the 15–29 age group has seen the largest absolute decrease — reflecting both the higher starting point and the impact of targeted violence reduction programs in some countries. However, the relative ranking of age groups has remained stable, with young adults consistently at highest risk.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: NOTABLE TRENDS & OUTLIERS ═══ -->
<h2 id="notable-trends">5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>

<p>While global and regional aggregates provide the big picture, some of the most important insights come from examining individual countries that have experienced dramatic changes — both improvements and deteriorations — in their homicide rates. This section compares average rates from the early 1990s (1990–1995) with the most recent period (2018–2023) for countries with data in both periods.</p>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart8_b64)}
</div>

<h3>Largest Improvements</h3>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Early Rate (1990–95)</th><th>Recent Rate (2018–23)</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{trends_table(R['notable_trends']['biggest_improvements'], 'improvement')}</tbody>
</table>

<p>Several countries have achieved remarkable reductions in homicide rates over the past three decades. These improvements often coincide with strengthened rule of law, economic development, demobilization following civil conflicts, and investment in community-based violence prevention. Countries emerging from periods of intense conflict or institutional instability have sometimes shown the most dramatic gains as baseline conditions normalize.</p>

<h3>Largest Deteriorations</h3>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Early Rate (1990–95)</th><th>Recent Rate (2018–23)</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{trends_table(R['notable_trends']['biggest_deteriorations'], 'deterioration')}</tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Warning:</strong> Countries experiencing the largest deteriorations often share common risk factors: political instability, weakening of judicial institutions, expansion of organized crime, and — in some cases — active conflict. These deteriorations are particularly concerning because reversal is difficult once violence becomes entrenched. Early intervention is critical.
</div>

<p>Some of the largest percentage increases occur in countries where the early-period baseline was very low, meaning that even small absolute increases translate into large percentage changes. Conversely, countries that started with very high rates and declined to moderate levels may show impressive absolute improvements even if the percentage change is less extreme. Both perspectives are important for policy interpretation.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: DATA LIMITATIONS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->
<h2 id="data-limitations">6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

<h3>Data Limitations</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart9_b64)}
</div>

<div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Critical Caveat:</strong> This dataset is an unbalanced panel with significant reporting gaps. Coverage has never exceeded {max(kpis['reporting_coverage']['by_year'].values()):.0f}% of countries in any year and stands at {kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.0f}% in the most recent year. This means global estimates are inherently incomplete and should be interpreted as indicative rather than definitive.
</div>

<ul style="margin: 16px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report data for every year. The set of reporting countries changes annually, making year-over-year comparisons of global aggregates potentially misleading. A shift in the average rate could reflect changes in which countries are reporting rather than genuine rate changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting gaps:</strong> Many countries — particularly those experiencing conflict or with limited statistical capacity — have sparse data. Their absence from the dataset may systematically bias global estimates downward, as these are often the countries with the highest actual homicide rates.</li>
    <li><strong>UK caveat:</strong> United Kingdom data refer to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately in the source data.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories vs. countries:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are included separately. These should not be double-counted with their parent countries in aggregate calculations.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing values have not been interpolated or imputed in this analysis. All reported figures derive directly from observed data.</li>
    <li><strong>Small-population volatility:</strong> Small island nations and territories can show extreme rates driven by a single incident, making their rates volatile from year to year.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

<ol style="margin: 16px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Prioritize the Americas and Caribbean:</strong> With regional median rates far exceeding the global average, these regions require sustained investment in violence prevention, law enforcement capacity, and addressing the root causes of organized crime.</li>
    <li><strong>Target young male populations:</strong> The 15–29 age group faces disproportionately high victimization. Programs focused on youth engagement, gang exit pathways, and economic opportunity for young men should be central to homicide reduction strategies.</li>
    <li><strong>Address gender-specific dimensions:</strong> While men face higher overall rates, female homicide patterns are often linked to intimate partner violence and require different intervention approaches. Both dimensions need tailored responses.</li>
    <li><strong>Invest in data infrastructure:</strong> The fact that only {kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.0f}% of countries report homicide data in any given year undermines global monitoring capacity. Strengthening national statistical systems, particularly in conflict-affected and low-income countries, is essential for evidence-based policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Act early on deteriorations:</strong> Countries showing rapid increases in homicide rates should receive priority attention. Once violence becomes entrenched, reversal is far more difficult and costly. Early warning systems based on trend monitoring can enable preventive action.</li>
    <li><strong>Context-specific strategies:</strong> The enormous variation in homicide rates across and within regions means that one-size-fits-all approaches will be ineffective. Policy responses should be calibrated to the specific drivers of violence in each context — whether organized crime, political instability, or socioeconomic deprivation.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect gains in improving countries:</strong> Countries that have achieved significant reductions should not become complacent. Sustaining improvements requires ongoing institutional commitment and adaptation to emerging threats.</li>
</ol>

<hr class="section-divider">

<h3>Methodology Note</h3>
<p>All statistics in this report are derived directly from the UNODC-based dataset ({R['overview']['total_rows']:,} observations across {R['overview']['unique_countries']} countries, {R['overview']['year_range'][0]}–{R['overview']['year_range'][1]}). The median is used as the primary central-tendency measure due to the highly right-skewed distribution of homicide rates. No values have been imputed or estimated. Regional classifications are based on standard UN geographic groupings. Decade-over-decade changes are computed using unweighted means of country-year observations within each decade.</p>

</div><!-- .content -->

<div class="footer">
    Global Intentional Homicide Trends Report | Data: UNODC Crime Trends Survey via Kaggle | Analysis period: 1990–2023 | Generated: 2026-05-21
</div>

</div><!-- .report-container -->
</body>
</html>
'''

