<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: NOTABLE TRENDS & OUTLIERS ═══ -->
<h2 id="notable-trends">5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>

<p>While global and regional aggregates provide the big picture, some of the most important insights come from examining individual countries that have experienced dramatic changes — both improvements and deteriorations — in their homicide rates. This section compares average rates from the early 1990s (1990–1995) with the most recent period (2018–2023) for countries with data in both periods.</p>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart8_b64)}
</div>

<h3>Largest Improvements</h3>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Early Rate (1990–95)</th><th>Recent Rate (2018–23)</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{trends_table(R['notable_trends']['biggest_improvements'], 'improvement')}</tbody>
</table>

<p>Several countries have achieved remarkable reductions in homicide rates over the past three decades. These improvements often coincide with strengthened rule of law, economic development, demobilization following civil conflicts, and investment in community-based violence prevention. Countries emerging from periods of intense conflict or institutional instability have sometimes shown the most dramatic gains as baseline conditions normalize.</p>

<h3>Largest Deteriorations</h3>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Early Rate (1990–95)</th><th>Recent Rate (2018–23)</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{trends_table(R['notable_trends']['biggest_deteriorations'], 'deterioration')}</tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Warning:</strong> Countries experiencing the largest deteriorations often share common risk factors: political instability, weakening of judicial institutions, expansion of organized crime, and — in some cases — active conflict. These deteriorations are particularly concerning because reversal is difficult once violence becomes entrenched. Early intervention is critical.
</div>

<p>Some of the largest percentage increases occur in countries where the early-period baseline was very low, meaning that even small absolute increases translate into large percentage changes. Conversely, countries that started with very high rates and declined to moderate levels may show impressive absolute improvements even if the percentage change is less extreme. Both perspectives are important for policy interpretation.</p>

