<!-- ═══ SECTION 6: DATA LIMITATIONS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══ -->
<h2 id="data-limitations">6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

<h3>Data Limitations</h3>

<div class="chart-wrap">
    {b64_img(chart9_b64)}
</div>

<div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Critical Caveat:</strong> This dataset is an unbalanced panel with significant reporting gaps. Coverage has never exceeded {max(kpis['reporting_coverage']['by_year'].values()):.0f}% of countries in any year and stands at {kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.0f}% in the most recent year. This means global estimates are inherently incomplete and should be interpreted as indicative rather than definitive.
</div>

<ul style="margin: 16px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report data for every year. The set of reporting countries changes annually, making year-over-year comparisons of global aggregates potentially misleading. A shift in the average rate could reflect changes in which countries are reporting rather than genuine rate changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting gaps:</strong> Many countries — particularly those experiencing conflict or with limited statistical capacity — have sparse data. Their absence from the dataset may systematically bias global estimates downward, as these are often the countries with the highest actual homicide rates.</li>
    <li><strong>UK caveat:</strong> United Kingdom data refer to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately in the source data.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories vs. countries:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are included separately. These should not be double-counted with their parent countries in aggregate calculations.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing values have not been interpolated or imputed in this analysis. All reported figures derive directly from observed data.</li>
    <li><strong>Small-population volatility:</strong> Small island nations and territories can show extreme rates driven by a single incident, making their rates volatile from year to year.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

<ol style="margin: 16px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Prioritize the Americas and Caribbean:</strong> With regional median rates far exceeding the global average, these regions require sustained investment in violence prevention, law enforcement capacity, and addressing the root causes of organized crime.</li>
    <li><strong>Target young male populations:</strong> The 15–29 age group faces disproportionately high victimization. Programs focused on youth engagement, gang exit pathways, and economic opportunity for young men should be central to homicide reduction strategies.</li>
    <li><strong>Address gender-specific dimensions:</strong> While men face higher overall rates, female homicide patterns are often linked to intimate partner violence and require different intervention approaches. Both dimensions need tailored responses.</li>
    <li><strong>Invest in data infrastructure:</strong> The fact that only {kpis['reporting_coverage']['latest_rate']:.0f}% of countries report homicide data in any given year undermines global monitoring capacity. Strengthening national statistical systems, particularly in conflict-affected and low-income countries, is essential for evidence-based policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Act early on deteriorations:</strong> Countries showing rapid increases in homicide rates should receive priority attention. Once violence becomes entrenched, reversal is far more difficult and costly. Early warning systems based on trend monitoring can enable preventive action.</li>
    <li><strong>Context-specific strategies:</strong> The enormous variation in homicide rates across and within regions means that one-size-fits-all approaches will be ineffective. Policy responses should be calibrated to the specific drivers of violence in each context — whether organized crime, political instability, or socioeconomic deprivation.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect gains in improving countries:</strong> Countries that have achieved significant reductions should not become complacent. Sustaining improvements requires ongoing institutional commitment and adaptation to emerging threats.</li>
</ol>

